---

<LegalLayout
  title="Client Resources & Document Checklists | Sulfab Law Office"
  description="Document checklists to help San Francisco and Bay Area clients prepare for a consultation, immigration court hearing, asylum filing, green card case, naturalization interview, or detention matter."
  h1="Client Resources & Document Checklists"
  eyebrow="Client Resources"
  lede="What to gather before a consultation, hearing, or filing. Organized by the type of case we see most often."
  updated="2026-11-21"
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    Immigration cases are usually decided on the documents. A complete record, with consistent dates
    and certified translations, prevents delays and protects options that can be lost when a filing
    is incomplete. The lists below are starting points, not exhaustive requirements — the documents
    that matter in your case depend on your history and the relief you are seeking.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bring <strong>copies</strong>, not originals, to a consultation. We will tell you when an original
    is required and how to transmit it safely. Do not send identification numbers, passport scans, or
    court records through the website’s general contact form.
  </p>

  <h2 id="before-your-first-consultation">Before your first consultation</h2>
  <p>For almost any matter, these items help us understand your situation quickly:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Photo identification</strong> — passport, state ID, or consular ID. A passport is
      especially important because it shows your entry stamp, visa, and any prior admissions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Immigration documents</strong> — visa, Form I-94, green card, employment authorization
      card, and any approval or denial notices you have received.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anything from the government</strong> — notices, court mail, letters from USCIS, ICE,
      or the Department of State, including envelopes with postmark dates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A short timeline</strong> — dates of entry, departures, prior applications, marriages,
      births, and any contact with immigration authorities.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Court documents</strong> — if you have ever been in immigration court or criminal
      court, bring the charging documents and dispositions, not just your memory of them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your questions</strong> — write them down. A consultation moves quickly, and the
      questions people forget are usually the ones that mattered most.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="removal-proceedings">Removal proceedings in immigration court</h2>
  <p>
    If you received a Notice to Appear (Form I-862) or a hearing notice from the immigration court,
    time is already running. Bring:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The Notice to Appear and every hearing notice, with the court name, date, and time.</li>
    <li>Your Alien Registration Number (A-number), if you have one.</li>
    <li>Any prior applications for relief and the government’s responses.</li>
    <li>Proof of your entries and departures, including passports and I-94 records.</li>
    <li>
      Evidence of ties to the United States — family relationships, children’s birth certificates,
      school records, employment, tax returns, property, and community involvement.
    </li>
    <li>
      Evidence of hardship to qualifying relatives if cancellation of removal, hardship waivers, or
      asylum-based relief may be relevant.
    </li>
    <li>
      Criminal records, if any: arrests, charging documents, plea agreements, judgments, and proof of
      completion. Do not omit these because they seem unfavorable — they are usually discoverable and
      affect strategy.
    </li>
    <li>Any prior deportation or removal orders, and proof of compliance if you departed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you have a hearing date, confirm it with the court’s automated system or the notice itself.
    Missing a hearing can result in an in-absentia removal order.
  </p>

  <h2 id="asylum-applications">Asylum applications</h2>
  <p>
    Asylum claims depend on a consistent, credible account tied to a protected ground. If you are
    preparing Form I-589, gather:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Passport, I-94, and any prior immigration filings or interviews.</li>
    <li>
      A detailed personal declaration in your own words — what happened, who did it, when, where, and
      why you believe it was because of race, religion, nationality, political opinion, or membership
      in a particular social group.
    </li>
    <li>
      Identity documents and any evidence of the harm: medical records, police reports, threatening
      messages, photographs, or witness statements.
    </li>
    <li>
      Evidence about your country — human rights reports, news articles, NGO reports, and
      country-condition expert declarations that address your specific group and region.
    </li>
    <li>
      Evidence that the government was unable or unwilling to protect you, if that is part of your
      claim.
    </li>
    <li>
      Documents for family members included in the application — birth certificates, marriage
      certificates, and proof of the relationship.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you are filing more than one year after arrival, evidence of changed or extraordinary
      circumstances that may excuse the late filing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The one-year filing deadline is strict. If you are close to it, contact an attorney before
    gathering every document — the filing itself may need to happen first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="family-green-cards">Family-based green cards and waivers</h2>
  <p>
    Family petitions and adjustment of status require proof of the relationship, the petitioner’s
    status, and admissibility. Typical documents include:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Relationship evidence</strong> — birth certificates for parent-child cases; marriage
      certificate and proof of a genuine marriage for spouse cases; divorce or death certificates
      ending prior marriages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Petitioner’s status</strong> — U.S. passport, birth certificate, naturalization
      certificate, or green card.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Beneficiary’s identity and entry</strong> — passport, visa, I-94, and any prior
      immigration filings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Financial support</strong> — Form I-864 with recent tax returns, W-2s, pay stubs, and
      proof of employment or assets. Joint sponsors may need the same documents.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Admissibility records</strong> — criminal dispositions, prior removals, overstays, and
      any immigration violations, because these determine whether a waiver is needed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Waiver evidence</strong> — for I-601 or I-601A cases, proof of qualifying relatives and
      the extreme hardship they would suffer if the applicant were refused admission.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Medical and civil documents</strong> — the Form I-693 medical exam (when required) and
      certified translations of foreign documents.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="naturalization">Naturalization (Form N-400)</h2>
  <p>For a naturalization application and interview, prepare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Permanent resident card and the date you became a permanent resident.</li>
    <li>Passport and a complete travel history for the statutory period, including exact dates.</li>
    <li>Tax returns and transcripts for the required years, plus proof of filing if you owe taxes.</li>
    <li>Marriage, divorce, and death certificates for all marriages, and evidence of any name change.</li>
    <li>Selective Service registration information, if applicable.</li>
    <li>
      Criminal records, if any, including arrests, citations, and dispositions — even for cases that
      were dismissed or expunged.
    </li>
    <li>Evidence of continuous residence and physical presence if you had long absences.</li>
    <li>
      For the civics test, study the current USCIS question list. For an English exemption or waiver,
      bring the medical certification (Form N-648) or other supporting evidence.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="detention-and-bond">Detention, bond hearings, and habeas</h2>
  <p>
    When someone is in ICE custody, the first hours matter. Have this information ready so we can
    locate the person and evaluate options quickly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The person’s full legal name, date of birth, country of birth, and A-number.</li>
    <li>The facility name and location, and any booking or detainee number.</li>
    <li>The date and place of the arrest or encounter, and any paperwork ICE left behind.</li>
    <li>Any prior immigration history — entries, removals, applications, and court cases.</li>
    <li>Criminal history, if any, including pending charges, because it affects bond eligibility.</li>
    <li>
      Community ties and a potential sponsor: identification, immigration status, address, income,
      and willingness to sign an affidavit and post bond.
    </li>
    <li>Medical conditions or safety concerns that require immediate attention.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For habeas corpus matters, also gather the detention and custody records, any bond decision, and
    the immigration court filings. If a transfer is planned, tell us immediately — jurisdiction and
    available relief can change with the facility.
  </p>

  <h2 id="practical-reminders">Practical reminders</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Translate everything.</strong> Documents in a foreign language must be accompanied by a
      complete English translation and a translator’s certification when filed with USCIS or a court.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep the envelopes.</strong> Postmarks and mailing dates can prove when a notice was
      sent and whether a deadline was met.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not guess on dates.</strong> Reconstruct your travel and case history from documents,
      not memory. Inconsistencies are one of the most common reasons cases run into trouble.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not discard old paperwork.</strong> Prior applications, denials, and court notices
      may be in government files even if you no longer have copies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tell us about criminal history, prior removals, and prior denials early.</strong> These
      facts shape strategy and are almost always discoverable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Watch deadlines.</strong> Filing windows for appeals, motions, and applications are
      short. A missed deadline can end a case regardless of its merits.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These checklists are general information, not legal advice. The documents required in your case
    depend on your facts and the relief you request. If you are unsure whether an item applies, bring
    it to the consultation and ask. You can also call the firm at
    <a href={firm.phoneHref}>{firm.phoneDisplay}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
